          position: 3,
          name: "Best Driveway Cleaners",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-driveway-cleaner/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best driveway cleaner?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Zep Driveway & Concrete Cleaner is the best overall driveway cleaner because it performs consistently across fresh oil, set-in stains, and tire marks on both asphalt and concrete. For fresh oil drips specifically, Simple Green Oil Stain Remover's poultice formula works even better. For years-old set-in stains, Krud Kutter Concrete & Driveway Cleaner/Degreaser's heavy-duty chemistry is the strongest performer we tested.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do you remove oil stains from a driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Apply a degreaser or oil-absorbing poultice directly to the stain, let it dwell for 10-20 minutes (or longer for enzyme-based cleaners), then scrub with a stiff nylon brush and rinse thoroughly with a hose or pressure washer. Fresh stains under a week old respond well to poultice products like Simple Green Oil Stain Remover. Older, set-in stains typically need a heavy-duty degreaser like Krud Kutter or Zep, sometimes with a second application.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best cleaner for old, set-in oil stains?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Krud Kutter Concrete & Driveway Cleaner/Degreaser performed best in our testing on stains that had set for years, thanks to its heavy-duty surfactant chemistry and extended dwell time. EF2000's bacterial bioremediation formula is also highly effective on old staining, though it works more slowly and typically needs multiple applications over several days.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to clean my driveway before sealing it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Sealer will not properly bond to a surface with oil, grease, dirt, or organic staining underneath it. Any contamination trapped beneath the sealer creates a weak spot that can peel, bubble, or fail early. Always degrease oil and grease stains, remove mildew and algae, and pressure wash the entire surface to remove loose dirt before applying any driveway sealer.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What's the difference between a degreaser and an oxygen bleach cleaner?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Degreasers use surfactants or enzymes to break down petroleum-based stains like oil, grease, and tar so they can be rinsed away. Oxygen bleach cleaners use an oxidizing reaction to lighten organic staining like mildew, algae, tannins, and general dinginess, but they are largely ineffective on petroleum oil stains. Most driveways benefit from a degreaser for oil spots and either oxygen bleach or a time-release cleaner like Wet & Forget for organic staining.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use dish soap or laundry detergent instead of a dedicated driveway cleaner?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Dish soap can help with very light, fresh grime but lacks the surfactant strength to break down petroleum-based oil and grease stains that have penetrated concrete or asphalt pores. Dedicated driveway cleaners and degreasers are formulated specifically to emulsify hydrocarbons and are far more effective, especially on set-in stains. Save dish soap for light surface dirt and use a purpose-built degreaser for actual oil and grease staining.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I remove rust stains from a concrete driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Rust stains require a different chemistry than oil stains because they're mineral, not petroleum-based. Standard degreasers like Zep or Krud Kutter will not remove rust effectively. Look for a cleaner or additive containing oxalic acid, or use a dedicated rust remover, applying it directly to the stain and following the dwell time on the label before rinsing. Acid-based cleaners should always be tested on a small, inconspicuous area first since they can affect certain decorative concrete finishes.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it safe to use driveway cleaner on asphalt?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most water-based degreasers, including Zep, Oil Eater, and EF2000, are safe on asphalt. However, some heavy-duty solvent-based degreasers can soften or discolor asphalt binder, so always check the product label for asphalt compatibility before applying. Krud Kutter Concrete & Driveway Cleaner/Degreaser, for example, is labeled for concrete use and is not recommended for raw asphalt. When in doubt, test any cleaner on a small, hidden section of asphalt first.",
