        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Driveways Stain -->
        <div id="why-clean-before-sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Driveways Stain (And Why Cleaning Matters Before Sealing)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both concrete and asphalt — the two dominant driveway surfaces — are <strong>porous</strong>. At a microscopic level, they're full of tiny capillaries that absorb whatever liquid lands on them. A drop of motor oil doesn't just sit on the surface; within hours it begins wicking down into those pores, and within days it starts to oxidize and chemically bond with the surrounding material. That's why a fresh oil spot lifts easily with a mild cleaner, while the same spot six months later requires a heavy-duty degreaser, extended dwell time, and real scrubbing effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rust stains come from a different source — usually dripping fluid from lawn furniture, planters, tools, or vehicle components — and they're mineral rather than petroleum-based, which is why standard degreasers barely touch them. Tire marks are a scuffing transfer of rubber compound rather than a liquid stain, and mildew, algae, and moss are living organisms that thrive in shaded, damp sections of a driveway. Each of these contamination types requires a different cleaning chemistry, which is exactly why a one-size-fits-all cleaner rarely handles everything a driveway throws at it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason this matters so much before sealing is simple: <strong>sealer bonds to the surface it's applied to, not to whatever is trapped underneath it.</strong> If you roll sealer over an oil stain, a patch of mildew, or a film of dirt and dust, you're not eliminating that contamination — you're locking it beneath a fresh layer of sealer where it continues to weaken the bond. Within a season, that section will typically show peeling, bubbling, or a permanent dark shadow bleeding through the new coating. Proper cleaning isn't a cosmetic nicety before sealing — it's the single most important step in making sure the sealer actually adheres and lasts its full expected lifespan.
          </p>

