        <!-- SECTION 2: Types of Cleaners -->
        <div id="types-of-cleaners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Driveway Cleaners: Which One Is Right for You?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>best driveway cleaner</strong> depends heavily on what's actually staining your surface — oil, rust, tire marks, or organic growth — as well as whether your driveway is asphalt or concrete. Here's how the major categories break down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Degreasers (Surfactant-Based)</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>Zep Driveway & Concrete Cleaner</strong>, <strong>Krud Kutter Concrete & Driveway Cleaner/Degreaser</strong>, and <strong>Oil Eater Original</strong> use surfactant chemistry to emulsify petroleum-based stains — motor oil, grease, transmission fluid, tar — so they lift free of the surface and rinse away. This is the most common category and the <strong>best concrete driveway cleaner</strong> starting point for most homeowners, since it handles the majority of everyday staining. Best for: general oil and grease stains ranging from fresh to moderately set.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oxygen Bleach Cleaners</h3>
          <p>
            Oxygen bleach-based products use an oxidizing chemical reaction rather than surfactants, which makes them excellent at lightening organic staining, tannin discoloration, and general dinginess without the harsh fumes of chlorine bleach or the surface risk of acid cleaners. They're a poor match for petroleum oil stains, since oxidation doesn't break down hydrocarbon chains the way a degreaser does, but they're a smart complement for driveways with mixed dirt-and-grime staining rather than isolated oil spots.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Enzyme / Bio-Based Cleaners</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>Simple Green Oil Stain Remover</strong>, <strong>EF2000 Concrete Cleaner Degreaser</strong>, and <strong>Star Brite Oil Stain Remover</strong> use enzymatic or bacterial bioremediation chemistry that actually consumes hydrocarbons rather than just lifting them off the surface. They tend to be gentler, safer around landscaping and septic systems, and — in the case of bioremediation formulas like EF2000 — remarkably effective on deeply set stains given enough dwell time. Best for: environmentally sensitive properties, septic systems, and stubborn stains where you're willing to trade speed for a more thorough result.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acid-Based Cleaners</h3>
          <p>
            Acid-based cleaners, typically built around muriatic or oxalic acid, are the category to reach for on <strong>rust stains</strong> and heavy mineral deposits that standard degreasers simply can't touch. They work by chemically dissolving the mineral bonds rather than emulsifying petroleum. They demand more caution — proper ventilation, protective gloves and eyewear, and testing on an inconspicuous patch first, since acid can etch or discolor certain decorative concrete finishes. Best for: rust stains, efflorescence, and mineral staining that degreasers leave untouched.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Concentrate vs. Ready-to-Use</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Concentrates</strong> like Oil Eater Original and EF2000 ship in a more potent form that you dilute yourself, which dramatically lowers the cost per treated square foot for anyone cleaning a full driveway rather than a single spot. <strong>Ready-to-use</strong> formats — including Zep's spray bottle option and Simple Green's poultice — trade some of that cost efficiency for convenience, letting you treat a single stain immediately without measuring or mixing. For whole-driveway cleaning ahead of sealing, a concentrate almost always makes more financial sense; for quick spot treatment of a fresh spill, ready-to-use wins on convenience.
          </p>

