        <!-- SECTION 5: How to Use -->
        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Use a Driveway Cleaner: The Complete Step-by-Step Guide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cleaning your own driveway before sealing is a realistic weekend project that can save you hundreds of dollars compared to hiring a pressure washing service. The process is straightforward, but <strong>dilution ratio, dwell time, and rinsing technique</strong> all matter — getting any one of them wrong can leave you with a streaky, unevenly cleaned surface that won't take sealer properly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Identify Your Stain Types</h3>
          <p>
            Walk the full driveway and note what you're dealing with — fresh oil drips, old set-in oil shadows, rust spots from planters or furniture, tire scuffing, and any dark, shaded patches of mildew or algae. Different stains need different chemistry, and identifying them upfront prevents you from wasting time and product using the wrong cleaner on the wrong stain, like trying to bleach out an oil stain or degrease a rust spot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Sweep and Pre-Rinse</h3>
          <p>
            Sweep away loose dirt, leaves, and debris, then pre-rinse the entire surface with a garden hose or pressure washer on a wide-fan setting. This removes surface grime so your cleaner's chemistry can focus on actual stains rather than being consumed by loose dirt.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Dilute (Or Don't) According to Stain Severity</h3>
          <p>
            For concentrates like <strong>Oil Eater</strong> or <strong>Zep</strong>, follow the label's dilution guidance — typically 1:4 to 1:10 with water for routine whole-driveway cleaning, and closer to 1:1 or full strength for isolated, stubborn stains. Over-diluting wastes your effort on tough spots; under-diluting on a whole-driveway wash wastes product and money without improving results. Ready-to-use products like <strong>Simple Green's</strong> poultice and <strong>Krud Kutter's</strong> spray need no mixing — apply directly as labeled.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Apply and Respect the Dwell Time</h3>
          <p>
            Apply the cleaner directly to the stain and let it dwell — this is the single most-skipped step and the biggest reason DIY cleaning underperforms. Light degreasers need 5-10 minutes; heavy-duty formulas like Krud Kutter need 15-20 minutes on tough stains; enzyme and bioremediation products like EF2000 need hours, and sometimes repeat applications over several days for deeply set staining. Don't let the product dry out completely on hot pavement — mist with water if needed to keep it active during the full dwell period.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Scrub With the Right Tool</h3>
          <p>
            A stiff nylon-bristle push broom works the cleaner into the surface texture without damaging decorative concrete finishes. For textured or stamped concrete, work in small circular motions to reach into the grooves. Avoid wire brushes on colored or stamped concrete, since they can scratch the finish.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 6: Pressure Wash and Rinse Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            A pressure washer dramatically improves results over a garden hose alone — use a 25-40 degree fan tip held 12-18 inches from the surface, working in overlapping passes from a consistent distance to avoid uneven "zebra striping." Rinse thoroughly until no suds or residue remain, and direct wash water away from garden beds and storm drains where required by your cleaner's label. Let the driveway dry completely — usually 24-48 hours depending on weather — before applying any sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Choosing by Stain & Surface -->
        <div id="choosing-by-stain-surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Choosing the Right Cleaner by Stain Type and Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>best driveway cleaner for your specific situation</strong> depends on exactly what kind of staining you're facing and whether your driveway is asphalt or concrete. Here's how to match the product to the problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fresh Oil Stains</h3>
          <p>
            For oil that's still fresh — hours to a few days old — <strong>Simple Green Oil Stain Remover's</strong> poultice format is the most effective option, pulling the oil out of the pores before it has a chance to bond permanently. <strong>Zep</strong> is a strong runner-up if you want a single all-purpose product rather than a dedicated poultice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Old / Set Oil Stains</h3>
          <p>
            For stains that have been sitting for months or years, <strong>Krud Kutter Concrete & Driveway Cleaner/Degreaser</strong> is the strongest performer thanks to its heavy-duty surfactant chemistry and longer dwell time. <strong>EF2000's</strong> bioremediation formula is the best environmentally responsible alternative, though it requires more patience and repeat applications.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tire Marks</h3>
          <p>
            Tire marks are rubber transfer rather than a liquid stain, and most degreasers — <strong>Zep</strong>, <strong>Oil Eater</strong>, and <strong>Krud Kutter</strong> — handle them well with a short dwell time and firm scrubbing, since the same surfactant chemistry that emulsifies oil also loosens rubber residue from the surface.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rust Stains</h3>
          <p>
            None of the degreasers on this list are formulated for rust, since it's a mineral stain rather than a petroleum one. Look specifically for a cleaner or additive containing oxalic acid, and always spot-test on an inconspicuous area first, since acid-based rust removers can affect certain decorative or colored concrete finishes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mildew, Algae & Organic Staining</h3>
          <p>
            For the dark, shaded staining that develops under trees or near gutters, <strong>Wet & Forget Outdoor's</strong> no-scrub, time-release formula is the least labor-intensive option, working over one to four weeks with just rainfall and dew. Oxygen bleach cleaners work faster if you want same-day results and are willing to scrub.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Asphalt vs. Concrete Considerations</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Asphalt</strong> driveways need water-based, non-solvent cleaners since some heavy solvent-based degreasers can soften or discolor the asphalt binder itself — <strong>Zep</strong>, <strong>Oil Eater</strong>, and <strong>EF2000</strong> are all safe choices. <strong>Concrete</strong> driveways, especially decorative stamped or colored finishes, tolerate a wider range of chemistry but should still avoid aggressive wire brushing. <strong>Krud Kutter</strong> is specifically labeled for concrete rather than raw asphalt, so check labels carefully if you have an asphalt surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best driveway cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Zep Driveway & Concrete Cleaner is the best overall driveway cleaner because it performs consistently across fresh oil, set-in stains, and tire marks on both asphalt and concrete. For fresh oil drips specifically, Simple Green Oil Stain Remover's poultice formula works even better. For years-old set-in stains, Krud Kutter Concrete & Driveway Cleaner/Degreaser's heavy-duty chemistry is the strongest performer we tested.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you remove oil stains from a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Apply a degreaser or oil-absorbing poultice directly to the stain, let it dwell for 10-20 minutes (or longer for enzyme-based cleaners), then scrub with a stiff nylon brush and rinse thoroughly with a hose or pressure washer. Fresh stains under a week old respond well to poultice products like Simple Green Oil Stain Remover. Older, set-in stains typically need a heavy-duty degreaser like Krud Kutter or Zep, sometimes with a second application.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cleaner for old, set-in oil stains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Krud Kutter Concrete & Driveway Cleaner/Degreaser performed best in our testing on stains that had set for years, thanks to its heavy-duty surfactant chemistry and extended dwell time. EF2000's bacterial bioremediation formula is also highly effective on old staining, though it works more slowly and typically needs multiple applications over several days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to clean my driveway before sealing it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Sealer will not properly bond to a surface with oil, grease, dirt, or organic staining underneath it. Any contamination trapped beneath the sealer creates a weak spot that can peel, bubble, or fail early. Always degrease oil and grease stains, remove mildew and algae, and pressure wash the entire surface to remove loose dirt before applying any driveway sealer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the difference between a degreaser and an oxygen bleach cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Degreasers use surfactants or enzymes to break down petroleum-based stains like oil, grease, and tar so they can be rinsed away. Oxygen bleach cleaners use an oxidizing reaction to lighten organic staining like mildew, algae, tannins, and general dinginess, but they are largely ineffective on petroleum oil stains. Most driveways benefit from a degreaser for oil spots and either oxygen bleach or a time-release cleaner like Wet & Forget for organic staining.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use dish soap or laundry detergent instead of a dedicated driveway cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dish soap can help with very light, fresh grime but lacks the surfactant strength to break down petroleum-based oil and grease stains that have penetrated concrete or asphalt pores. Dedicated driveway cleaners and degreasers are formulated specifically to emulsify hydrocarbons and are far more effective, especially on set-in stains. Save dish soap for light surface dirt and use a purpose-built degreaser for actual oil and grease staining.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I remove rust stains from a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rust stains require a different chemistry than oil stains because they're mineral, not petroleum-based. Standard degreasers like Zep or Krud Kutter will not remove rust effectively. Look for a cleaner or additive containing oxalic acid, or use a dedicated rust remover, applying it directly to the stain and following the dwell time on the label before rinsing. Acid-based cleaners should always be tested on a small, inconspicuous area first since they can affect certain decorative concrete finishes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use driveway cleaner on asphalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most water-based degreasers, including Zep, Oil Eater, and EF2000, are safe on asphalt. However, some heavy-duty solvent-based degreasers can soften or discolor asphalt binder, so always check the product label for asphalt compatibility before applying. Krud Kutter Concrete & Driveway Cleaner/Degreaser, for example, is labeled for concrete use and is not recommended for raw asphalt. When in doubt, test any cleaner on a small, hidden section of asphalt first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

